# RX_PCGTest — erosion surface-volume prototype

> Historical box-based prototype. Its box actors, box Blueprint assets and old
> PCG graphs have been replaced by the spline prototype documented in
> [../rx_pcg_spline/README.md](../rx_pcg_spline/README.md). The scripts and evidence
> below describe the former implementation; do not rerun them to edit the current
> spline assets. `bridge.py`, `open_verified_level.py` and `save_prototype.py`
> remain shared editor utilities.

Open `/Game/Study/RX_PCGTest/RXTestMap`. The saved selection is Erosion1.
Implementation is Blueprint + native UE 5.6 PCG. Python is editor authoring and
verification only; no project C++ was added or changed.

Naming review: all 54 assets, 15 Blueprints and their graphs use English
identifiers. The 200 custom variables and 16 custom enum entries have Korean
tooltips. E_Erosion values remain 0/1/2, preserving existing Blueprint references.
Python authoring uses `EROSION1`/`EROSION2`/`EROSION3` after an editor restart.
`naming_saved_verification.json` records the saved-asset readback.
The review also corrected unsaved raycast override flags in PCG environments
1 and 3; `finalize_volume_graphs.py` now forces the graph assets to disk.

| Environment | Lighting | Include box | Exclude box | Default instances |
|---|---|---|---|---:|
| Erosion1 | Day | Red | Pink | 600 |
| Erosion2 | Twilight | Green | Yellow | 600 |
| Erosion3 | Evening | Blue | Cyan | 600 |

Each environment has seven include boxes (four wall patches, three pillar
patches) and five exclude boxes. Positions use a fixed seed; reopening does not
randomize the layout. Exclude outlines are thicker.

## Editing areas

### Meshes, weights, spacing and count

Select **ErosionLayout** in RXPart. Each **Erosion1/2/3** category exposes:

| Control suffix | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| Meshes | Chamfered cone and block | Static Mesh array; add/remove individual choices |
| MeshWeights | [3, 1] | Relative weight at the matching mesh-array index; zero excludes a choice |
| Spacing | 160 cm | Sampling grid spacing; larger values reduce surface candidates |
| Count | 600 | Maximum generated count; zero disables generation |

Keep the mesh and weight arrays the same length and at least one weight positive.
Spacing controls candidate sampling rather than a guaranteed minimum distance.
Count is limited by the available surface candidates after include/exclude masks
and pruning. All three native graphs read their own environment's controls from
the same layout reference in the editor and runtime.

### Random scatter, clusters and overlap removal

Every erosion has 62 editable controls on **ErosionLayout**, grouped by purpose.
The six main scatter controls are:

| Control suffix | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| ScatterJitter | 200 cm | Randomize candidate XYZ positions before projecting onto the surface |
| OverlapRadius | 40 cm | Half-size of each pruning box; intersecting boxes are removed |
| ClusterSize | 700 cm | Approximate length over which coherent density changes |
| ClusterContrast | 3 | Higher makes dense centers and empty patches more distinct; lower softens the fade |
| ClusterDensity | 0.2 | Brightness offset of the density field; lower produces more empty space |
| ScatterSeed | 17311 | Repeatable candidate, density and count-selection pattern |

Keep ScatterJitter at least comparable to Spacing. Setting it to zero restores
grid rows. ScatterAxisScale controls how much each axis jitters. The tilted noise
projection varies density across wall width and height, rather than producing
vertical stripes. Density becomes a survival probability: points thin gradually
away from dense regions. Seed sorting before Count prevents traversal-order rows.

OverlapRadius is a point-clearance control implemented with native Bounds Modifier
and Self Pruning. The test radius of 40 cm gave minimum center separation above
80 cm in all three environments. Increasing it to 150 cm gave separation above
300 cm. Choose a radius large enough for the mesh's maximum cross-section and
ScaleMax; arbitrary mesh geometry and later rotation/offset are not collision-tested.

Additional controls cover axis sampling, four world trace directions, trace length,
line/box/sphere/capsule sweeps, object collision channel, detailed collision,
backface/self-hit rules, all five cluster noise modes, iterations, projection,
tiling, Voronoi settings and edge-fade curves. Pruning exposes its mode, randomized
priority and similarity threshold. Transform includes local offset ranges,
absolute/local switches and a separate variation seed. Rendering includes the
first-slot MaterialOverride and instance merging. Advanced settings are grouped
separately. Korean tooltips describe mode-dependent settings and units.

SurfaceCollisionChannel is an **object type**, default WorldStatic. Visibility
is not a useful default for these object queries. Minimum/maximum ranges must
stay ordered; axis spacing and scale must stay positive. Surface offsets apply
after mask clipping, so verify clearance after changing them.

## Detailed architecture

Main has six instanced mesh groups: one chamfered floor, four brick walls,
three slim pillars, two medium pillars, two wide pillars, and 17 TV modules.
The pillar cores are 260x280, 400x360, and 560x480 cm, with chamfered collars,
plinths, and vertical ribs. Every column has two inward-facing screens; the
three interior columns have a third screen. TVs include glass, casing, indicator,
vent slits, and a mounting bracket. Brick faces and structural hard edges use
actual beveled geometry. The editable PLA source is `Main_Geometry` and contains
the corresponding 29 mesh actors. RXPart's existing Main actor identity and its
SurfaceTarget reference are retained.

`detail_room_final.png` and `detail_tv_final.png` render the authored geometry
with temporary review lights; these lights are removed after capture.

## Per-environment rotation and scale

Select **ErosionLayout** in RXPart and expand **Erosion1/2/3 > Transform**.
The Blueprint instance controls feed the corresponding native PCG graph in
both the editor preview and the runtime environment:

| Control suffix | Type | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| RotationMin / RotationMax | Rotator | 0 / 0 degrees | Random local rotation added to the surface-aligned orientation |
| ScaleMin / ScaleMax | Vector | (0.85, 0.85, 0.85) / (1.3, 1.3, 1.3) | Random multiplicative XYZ scale |
| UniformScale | Boolean | true | Use the X range for one shared scale on all axes; false samples XYZ separately |

Variable names have the environment prefix, e.g. `Erosion1RotationMin`.
Keep each minimum at or below its maximum. Equal endpoints give a fixed value.
Scale values must be positive. Names/categories are English; tooltips are Korean.
After editing, use **Refresh Part Preview** (or **Refresh Preview** in RXTestMap),
then save RXPart or commit its Level Instance edit. Larger scales can overlap
neighbors or extend across volume boundaries: masks test attachment positions,
and the existing self-pruning happens before the transform variation.

`transform_controls_verification.json` checks all three environments with fixed
rotation/scale, random per-axis ranges, and uniform scaling against actual ISM
transforms. `transform_runtime_verification.json` compares generated editor and
PIE transforms. Tests restore the prior instance settings.

## Include and exclude boxes

1. Open RXPart, or edit its Level Instance in RXTestMap.
2. Find ErosionLayout and the ErosionVolumes/Erosion1..3 Outliner folders.
3. Move, rotate, or scale ErosionN_Include_* and ErosionN_Exclude_* actors.
4. Duplicate a box to add a region. Keep it attached to the same ErosionLayout.
   For a fresh actor, use BP_ErosionN_Include or BP_ErosionN_Exclude and attach it
   to that layout in the Outliner.
5. Set Mapsetting.Erosion. In RXPart, click **Refresh Part Preview** on
   EditorPreview_RXPart. In RXTestMap, click **Refresh Preview** on
   EditorEnvironmentPreview to update both PCG and lighting.
6. Save RXPart or commit the Level Instance edit. Refreshing a loaded instance
   alone does not save changes into the source level.

Blueprint classes identify environment/purpose; constituent actor tags inside
the PLA are not used. Hide other erosion folders when their boxes obscure the
selected environment. Boxes are hidden in game and have no collision.

## Structure

RXPart contains the Main PLA, BP_PCGManager, EditorPreview_RXPart, ErosionLayout,
and 36 volume actors. ErosionLayout.SurfaceTarget references Main directly.
The manager and preview reference their own Layout. Attached region actors
provide its scope. Level Instance duplication remaps these local references.

Mapsetting retains three soft class references. The manager loads the selected
lighting sublevel, asynchronously loads one environment BP, and spawns it with
Owner set to the manager. That BP copies the manager's Layout before Generate.

Each native PCG graph reads its Layout, unions its include boxes, samples at
the configured Spacing multiplied by SamplingAxisScale, randomizes candidate
positions, and casts four default horizontal directions up to 700 cm. Only hits
on SurfaceTarget are accepted; generated PCG meshes are ignored. Hit points use
the actual surface normal. Intersection clips them to include areas, then the
union of exclusions takes precedence. A tilted coherent noise field controls
stochastic survival. Self Pruning with configurable half-bounds removes overlaps
and duplicates. Blueprint transform overrides feed Transform Points. Random seed
sorting precedes the count cap and weighted mesh matching. Static
Mesh Spawner creates an ISM per chosen mesh, with no generated-mesh collision.

Lighting sublevels contain only dynamic lighting: day, twilight, evening. Only
one is loaded/visible at runtime. The editor preview generates only the selected
PCG component and deactivates/cleans the others to prevent editor dependency
refreshes from regenerating unselected environments. It is editor-only for cooking and destroys
itself in PIE; the runtime manager creates the real environment instance.

