## Limits and next steps

Counts follow usable surface area, sampling density, and exclusions. Small or
fully excluded regions do not force 200 cones. This layout exceeds 200 in every
environment. Sampling uses a world grid, so rotating an RXPart can change the
count. The sampling grid is jittered before projection, so generated rows are
not aligned even though the initial candidate budget uses voxel sampling.

The four rays support wall and pillar sides. Floors/ceilings need vertical rays.
Target meshes need query collision; this architecture uses complex collision.
Exclusion tests the attachment point, not the complete cone silhouette: enlarge
boxes for clearance around doors/player routes. Keep actor scale positive.

One-shot generation and instancing reduce ongoing cost; async loading does not
remove generation cost. Packaged cold-load time, memory and frame time have not
been benchmarked. Large volumes or fine spacing need a candidate-point budget
before production use.

Authoring scripts include exploratory steps and are not one rerunnable pipeline.
Saved Unreal assets are the deliverable. The earlier fixed 360-point graphs have
been replaced by surface-volume graphs.
